# Force LF regardless of the checking-out machine's core.autocrlf. This repo's
# git installs default to core.autocrlf=true (Windows), which silently
# rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout for anything git treats as text -- fine for
# most files, but fatal for scripts a Linux container executes with bash:
# a CRLF line ending becomes a literal trailing \r in every unquoted
# expansion on that line, so `cd "$APP_ROOT/gateway"` actually tries to `cd`
# into a path with an invisible \r appended and fails, `set -uo pipefail`
# breaks the same way, etc. This silently broke the Azure deploy's
# startup.sh once already (CRLF crept back in after a Windows edit) --
# every `cd` in it failed, so the gateway process launched from the wrong
# directory and Azure showed its "Hey, Python developers!" default page
# instead of a real error.
*.sh text eol=lf
.deployment text eol=lf
